{"name":"Wakefield","hex":"#295468","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Wakefield","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An industrial city and metropolitan borough of West Yorkshire, England; traditionally a centre for the cloth and coal industries.","A habitational surname from Old English."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wakefield"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wakefield","blocks":[{"text":"Wakefield is a cathedral city in West Yorkshire, England, located on the River Calder. The city had a population of 109,766 in the 2021 census, up from 99,251 in the 2011 census. The city is the administrative centre of the wider Metropolitan Borough of Wakefield, which had a 2025 population of 369,450, the 26th most populous district in England. It is part of the West Yorkshire Built-up Area and the Yorkshire and the Humber region."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/WakefieldCathedral-1.jpg/330px-WakefieldCathedral-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wakefield","wikidata":"Q216638"},{"title":"Wakefield (constituency)","blocks":[{"text":"Wakefield was a constituency in West Yorkshire, England. It was created as a borough constituency in 1832 and reformed as a county constituency in 1885."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Wakefield2007Constituency.svg/330px-Wakefield2007Constituency.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wakefield_(constituency)","wikidata":"Q874488"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Wakefield%2C_industry_on_George_Street%2C_from_the_railway_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7652134.jpg/500px-Wakefield%2C_industry_on_George_Street%2C_from_the_railway_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7652134.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb